{
    "title": "Fourth Temporary Extension of COVID-19 Telemedicine Flexibilities for Prescription of Controlled Medications",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 31, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-31/pdf/2025-24123.pdf",
    "summary": "The federal government issued several year-end updates: the Drug Enforcement Administration, working with the Department of Health and Human Services, is extending pandemic-era telemedicine flexibilities for prescribing controlled medicines through December 31, 2026; the Securities and Exchange Commission streamlined an internal process for handling confidentiality requests; and the Coast Guard set a short-term safety zone for a fireworks event in Long Beach, California.\n\nThe telemedicine extension is the headline change. It keeps in place the ability for licensed clinicians to prescribe controlled medicines to patients via telemedicine, even if they have not met in person, so long as other laws are followed. The agencies say this avoids a sudden cutoff in access to care while they finish permanent rules and give doctors, clinics, pharmacies, and technology platforms time to adjust. They point to recent drops in virtual care when other flexibilities lapsed as a sign that an abrupt end could disrupt treatment.\n\nWho benefits? Patients who rely on remote care\u2014especially those in rural areas, people with mobility or transportation challenges, and those receiving treatment for conditions like anxiety, ADHD, and opioid use disorder\u2014are most likely to see continuity. Clinicians and clinics that built telemedicine services during the pandemic gain time to plan for long-term requirements. Technology providers and pharmacies also get a clearer runway to prepare for whatever permanent rules follow.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Some public health and safety advocates worry extended flexibility could increase the risk of inappropriate prescribing or diversion. In\u2011person practices could see fewer visits tied to initial evaluations. And businesses still face uncertainty about the final shape of permanent telemedicine rules, which could require new workflows or registrations.\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission\u2019s action is narrower: it delegated to the Director of the Division of Investment Management the authority to grant, deny, or revoke confidential treatment for certain investment adviser filings. The Commission can still review any decision, but the change is designed to speed internal processing and does not add new obligations for firms. This should help advisers seeking timely confidentiality, though some may prefer decisions at the Commission level.\n\nLastly, the Coast Guard created a temporary safety zone around the Carnival Cruise Terminal pier in Long Beach to protect people and vessels during a fireworks display, which means brief, localized limits on boat traffic for safety.",
